MARK MANDEL: So you
said a lot of words

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:36.835
that I'm going to guess
relate to big data and stuff.

00:08:36.835 --> 00:08:38.460
But why don't we
start at a high level.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:40.470
So what is Informatica
actually do?

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:42.960
BILL CREEKBAUM: Informatica
provides a portfolio

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.000
of data management products.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.220
All of our products are
very metadata aware,

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:52.350
and the benefit there is by
having a complete solution that

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:56.910
focuses on integration,
governance, security, data

00:08:56.910 --> 00:09:00.120
quality, and discoverability--

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.370
you have that completely
integrated solution

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:06.330
that allows you to move
quickly, move safely, deal

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:09.220
with strict regulations
with confidence.

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:12.120
At the same time, deliver
business value, right?

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:15.600
I mean, everyone talks about
the democratization of data,

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:16.863
this is how you do it.

00:09:16.863 --> 00:09:18.780
If you're missing any
one of those pieces then

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:19.935
you're starting to worry.

00:09:19.935 --> 00:09:21.690
You open yourselves
up to risk and then

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.530
that's when you start
to hold the data back.

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:25.630
And so I think
with our platform,

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:28.585
we really allow business users
to get a hold of the data.

00:09:28.585 --> 00:09:29.460
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:31.800
So what's a really
great example of someone

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:34.098
using Informatica and
Google Cloud together

00:09:34.098 --> 00:09:34.890
that people might--

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:36.840
like what would be a
canonical example of that?

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:38.620
BILL CREEKBAUM: There's a
couple of really good examples.

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:41.250
I think a lot of what I've been
really excited to see recently

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:44.910
is customers that try to
build like a customer 360

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:46.740
type analytical solutions.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.230
And they started on-prem,
they started using [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:52.848
and that was really a lot of
the promise of the beginning

00:09:52.848 --> 00:09:53.890
of the big data movement.

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:57.420
But the operational costs
are just so extreme.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:00.510
But now they're working
with Google and BigQuery

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:03.150
in particular, and
they're just seeing

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:05.460
so much tremendous
opportunity to move

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:10.140
those very expensive workloads
on-prem into the cloud.

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:11.610
They're saving a ton of money.

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:14.560
But more importantly, they're
seeing new opportunities.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:19.128
The technology is opening
doors to new use cases.

00:10:19.128 --> 00:10:21.420
And I think that's my favorite
part, is customers begin

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:22.878
the journey as
like, oh, it's going

00:10:22.878 --> 00:10:24.990
to be cheaper for me
to run it in the cloud.

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:28.150
And then they pause and they
go like, Wait, wait, wait.

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:31.920
You mean, I can do it
completely different and faster

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:33.840
and still do it cheaper?

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:36.480
That's when you start to see
the creativity of our customers

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:40.000
in our industry at large,
really take flight.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:44.770
GABI FERRARA: So Informatica,
from what I got it,

00:10:44.770 --> 00:10:50.930
you were able to facilitate
ETL processing for customers.

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:53.310
Let's say you let them
build their own pipelines,

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:54.070
apparently.

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:59.530
And beyond that, how the
customers approach you?

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:02.950
You said that some already
have an old set up already

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:04.700
from the beginning of big data.

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:07.390
But how usually a
new company comes

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:09.460
to you that has no
big data at all,

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:11.320
they want to start doing it--

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:13.432
how does that approach happen?

00:11:13.432 --> 00:11:14.890
BILL CREEKBAUM:
Well, it definitely

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:17.050
depends on where that
particular customer is

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:20.480
in their-- let's call it the
modernization journey, right?

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:22.420
But what we've been
really excited to see

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:26.140
is that, when our customers
use our complete portfolio, not

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:28.850
just our integration products
but the complete portfolio,

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:32.650
especially with our metadata
driven catalog capabilities.

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:35.210
Because they start much more
from a use case perspective,

00:11:35.210 --> 00:11:37.810
they start from a business
object perspective.

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:41.220
Because first of all, you got
to find the right data, right?

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:43.830
You might have a sales
operation use case, right?

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:45.550
And you're working
with sales ops.

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:47.680
You want to
modernize, let's say,

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:50.590
a marketing lead campaign
initiative, right?

00:11:50.590 --> 00:11:53.750
Well, to start, "I got to find
good marketing lead data."

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:57.640
And so if I have a catalog that
has scanning my enterprise,

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:00.430
I can type in the Google
for the enterprise, right?

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:02.690
Show me all my
marketing lead data.

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:06.700
And you might get back 20, 30,
maybe 50 different data assets.

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:09.250
At that point, OK, I'm
overwhelmed as a business user.

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:10.950
"So do I just pick
the one at the top?

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:12.140
Do it randomly pick?

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:13.630
How do I choose?"

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:15.670
And what's really
beneficial is that when

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:18.550
you have that data catalog
capability with a higher order

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:19.280
value--

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:24.070
use machine learning to identify
business objects, locate PII.

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:27.430
You can start to make some
really intelligent decisions.

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:30.380
Because now I can determine,
this says marketing lead.

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:32.170
Does it have all
the elements that's

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:35.540
required for a complete
marketing lead object?

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:36.490
So that's step one.

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:38.710
And then step two
is, "do I know where

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:40.965
that data came
from and do I know

00:12:40.965 --> 00:12:42.340
if it's being used
downstream and

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:43.640
by a different application?"

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:47.050
So for example, if I see
a marketing lead object

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:51.910
and I see that that object
is being populated by Marketo

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:55.000
and Salesforce sources, right?

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.640
Those are pretty reliable
industry sources.

00:12:57.640 --> 00:13:00.850
And I see it being consumed by
10 downstream applications that

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:04.270
says, wow, this is actually
being used by the business.

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:08.330
I can safely assume that this
object is valuable and trusted.

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:10.480
Whereas, if I compare it
with another object that

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:12.850
has no downstream
consumption, I can

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:16.540
make some informed decisions
on which data to begin using.

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:20.620
So the point is, is by combining
data discovery with data

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:22.840
integration, you can make
much better decisions

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:24.237
about what data to integrate.

00:13:24.237 --> 00:13:26.320
And then go straight into
the provisioning process

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:27.280
to BigQuery.

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:29.800
GABI FERRARA: I usually see
people saying, and I agree,

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:33.670
that [? the business file ?]
relies on the quality of data

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:34.255
that you have.

00:13:34.255 --> 00:13:36.880
It doesn't matter how much money
you're bringing, if you're not

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:39.520
creating your data
properly, you're

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:41.950
going to have some
troubles, especially

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:43.310
if you want to modernize.

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:46.550
Do data analytics,
do machine learning,

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:49.160
you need to rely on your source.

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:51.640
And do you encounter
a lot of people

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:57.940
having problems with data that's
not normalized, duplicated

00:13:57.940 --> 00:13:58.760
records?

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:01.960
Let's say, do you
have that many issues

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:05.400
and do you help those customers
solve this kind of issues

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:06.265
too or not?

00:14:06.265 --> 00:14:07.390
BILL CREEKBAUM: Absolutely.

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:09.348
I've been in the analytics
space for the better

00:14:09.348 --> 00:14:12.760
part of the past decade and
I've seen so many analytics

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:16.277
initiatives fail after such
great intentions because

00:14:16.277 --> 00:14:17.110
of bad quality data.

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:19.068
It has nothing to do with
the analytics itself,

00:14:19.068 --> 00:14:21.263
it has to do with bad
raw material, right?

00:14:21.263 --> 00:14:22.930
And the quality data
comes from a couple

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:23.888
of different scenarios.

00:14:23.888 --> 00:14:26.320
Number one is, is it safe
for me to access, right?

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:30.238
If I can't safely access
the data, there's no point.

00:14:30.238 --> 00:14:31.780
At that point quality
doesn't matter.

00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:37.300
So having a solution that masks
data or adheres to role-based

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:38.860
access controls--

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:42.460
number one, I mean, that allows
a business user to safely

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:43.380
use the data, right?

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:46.240
And we all talk about
enabling the citizen x, right?

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:48.690
Citizen integrators, citizen
application developers,

00:14:48.690 --> 00:14:49.750
citizen analysts.

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:53.650
We want those business users
to safely acquire them.

00:14:53.650 --> 00:14:57.280
So once your platform has
allowed them to safely acquire

00:14:57.280 --> 00:14:59.400
the data, what is the quality?

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:02.410
And quality is completeness
of data, right?

00:15:02.410 --> 00:15:05.410
If I've got a data set that's
got a bunch of null values,

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:07.360
that's going to have
limited utility for me.

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:09.610
And being able to really
profile your data set

00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:11.710
gives me an understanding
of the quality of data.

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:16.150
But beyond that, now we get into
a higher order of data quality.

00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:19.290
And so let's continue our
marketing lead example.

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:22.690
If I've got a table with
really good marketing lead

00:15:22.690 --> 00:15:26.590
object definitions and part of
that is email addresses, phone

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:30.490
numbers, and zip codes, yet
60% of my email addresses

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:34.740
are malformed and my zip
codes only have three digits--

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:37.130
yeah, the structure
might look fantastic,

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:40.070
but the actual data is going
to be useless for me, right?

00:15:40.070 --> 00:15:41.870
And you don't want
to throw it away.

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:43.850
Do you have the ability
to recover from it

00:15:43.850 --> 00:15:46.110
and get quality data?

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:49.072
And if not, can we
safely exclude the data?

00:15:49.072 --> 00:15:51.530
So it's absolutely essential
because at the end of the day,

00:15:51.530 --> 00:15:54.650
analytics comes down to,
do you trust the output?

00:15:54.650 --> 00:15:57.800
And the higher the quality
input, the better trust you

00:15:57.800 --> 00:15:59.217
have with a particular analytic.

00:15:59.217 --> 00:16:01.550
MARK MANDEL: Given that we
are the Google Cloud Platform

00:16:01.550 --> 00:16:03.140
podcast, this all
sounds really great.

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:06.080
But in application, what
tools and products are we

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:08.992
using here on Google Cloud to
help facilitate this pipeline?

00:16:08.992 --> 00:16:10.450
BILL CREEKBAUM:
Well, our customers

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:12.950
is a combination of both our
company's products, right?

00:16:12.950 --> 00:16:13.280
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:15.380
BILL CREEKBAUM: And so,
from Informatica's side,

00:16:15.380 --> 00:16:18.235
they're using our
intelligent cloud services,

00:16:18.235 --> 00:16:20.360
they're using our enterprise
data catalog, big data

00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:22.880
management, and sometimes
security source, which

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:24.860
is one of our governance tools.

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:29.360
And then on the Google Cloud
Platform side, Google BigQuery.

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:33.230
I mean, I'm telling you guys, I
am seeing so much traffic flow

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:34.820
through to BigQuery.

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:37.360
And the adoption is fantastic.

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:41.060
And I'm seeing huge
curves up and to the right

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:43.680
on adoption and usage.

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:45.530
But to really
complement BigQuery,

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:47.570
there's a lot of usage
of Google Cloud Storage.

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:48.810
That makes less sense.

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:52.040
I mean, behind the covers, we
use [? it a lot ?] in tandem

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:55.460
to really drive performance
of bulk loading.

00:16:55.460 --> 00:16:57.350
We also see a lot
of inner operation

00:16:57.350 --> 00:16:59.060
with Google Analytics.

00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:02.270
And we're starting to see a much
bigger uptake of technologies

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:05.339
like Pub/Sub as part
of these workflows.

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:07.220
And I already talked
about Dataproc earlier.

00:17:07.220 --> 00:17:09.430
So one of things that I
think about a great deal,

00:17:09.430 --> 00:17:10.930
and that my entire
team thinks about

00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:14.210
is, how can we best
provide our customers

00:17:14.210 --> 00:17:18.079
with the strengths of BigQuery
and the entire Google Cloud

00:17:18.079 --> 00:17:22.422
platform surfaced through the
Informatica products, right?

00:17:22.422 --> 00:17:24.339
Because I want to give
them easy access to it.

00:17:24.339 --> 00:17:26.535
That's what Informatica
brings to the table, right?

00:17:26.535 --> 00:17:29.030
We bring the easy access,
visual design environments,

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:33.030
governance, lineage, metadata
management, et cetera.

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:35.420
But I don't want to provide
coverage that's just like,

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:36.680
vanilla access.

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:38.712
I want our customers
to take the most

00:17:38.712 --> 00:17:40.170
advantage of why
they picked Google

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:41.720
Cloud in the first place.

00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:42.470
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:17:42.470 --> 00:17:43.920
Do you have customers
that are like,

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:45.600
"hey, I've already got
a Google Cloud project

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:47.570
and I've got a bunch of stuff
to Google Cloud storage."

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:49.190
And they come to you and they're
like, "we're already doing

00:17:49.190 --> 00:17:50.150
stuff, how do we integrate?"

00:17:50.150 --> 00:17:51.740
Or do you find it's
coming from the other way

00:17:51.740 --> 00:17:53.540
where you've got
customers for Informatica,

00:17:53.540 --> 00:17:54.470
and they're like,
"oh, actually now

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:56.752
we want to push this
down into Google Cloud

00:17:56.752 --> 00:17:57.960
in some way, shape, or form."

00:17:57.960 --> 00:17:58.910
BILL CREEKBAUM: I got to
tell you, Mark, sometimes I

00:17:58.910 --> 00:18:01.243
feel like it's coming from
top, bottom, left, and right.

00:18:01.243 --> 00:18:02.050
[LAUGHS]

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:03.682
And all different combinations.

00:18:03.682 --> 00:18:05.390
But in all seriousness,
I mean, we really

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:07.580
focus on the hybrid
enterprise, right?

00:18:07.580 --> 00:18:10.978
And there's just crazy
momentum to the cloud.

00:18:10.978 --> 00:18:13.520
I mean, heck, I've been talking
about it ever since the cloud

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:14.630
was born, right?

00:18:14.630 --> 00:18:18.500
But it is just happening
with fierce velocity.

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:20.870
But no matter that
velocity, large enterprises

00:18:20.870 --> 00:18:22.760
are still going to have
a presence on-prem.

00:18:22.760 --> 00:18:26.450
And we have to treat that
hybrid nature of on-prem, cloud,

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:28.710
multi-cloud as first
class citizens.

00:18:28.710 --> 00:18:29.880
We all do.

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:33.020
So a lot of times
our customers will--

00:18:33.020 --> 00:18:36.410
I hate to speak in generalities,
but from anecdotal engagements

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:38.030
and evidence, a lot
of our customers

00:18:38.030 --> 00:18:41.275
we'll start with, "I need
to modernize my warehouse

00:18:41.275 --> 00:18:42.650
and I want to get
into BigQuery."

00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:45.922
Great cost, performance,
benefits, great technology.

00:18:45.922 --> 00:18:48.380
And they start off with really
just more of a cost savings.

00:18:48.380 --> 00:18:51.530
They want to get rid of their
physical infrastructure,

00:18:51.530 --> 00:18:54.170
shift the operation
load to Google.

00:18:54.170 --> 00:18:56.900
But the reality is that
halfway through that journey,

00:18:56.900 --> 00:18:59.600
that light, that
epiphany happens.

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:04.130
They start to see much more the
value add of what can be done.

00:19:04.130 --> 00:19:07.130
For example, I was engaging
with a large joint customer

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:09.080
earlier in the year,
and their initial goal

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:12.440
was just to modernize their
warehouse with BigQuery.

00:19:12.440 --> 00:19:15.290
We started down that path
and it was going really well.

00:19:15.290 --> 00:19:16.970
And actually during
a joint engagement

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:19.250
where I was actually
co-presenting with somebody

00:19:19.250 --> 00:19:22.220
from Google to this particular
customer, the light went on

00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:23.720
and they're going,
"wait, wait wait.

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:26.750
Can I replace my Hadoop
environment on-prem

00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:30.618
with BigQuery and actually
unify all my data?"

00:19:30.618 --> 00:19:31.910
You should have seen the grins.

00:19:31.910 --> 00:19:34.430
It was like this
collective room of lights

00:19:34.430 --> 00:19:36.750
going on about
what was possible.

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:38.770
The art of the possible.

00:19:38.770 --> 00:19:43.940
So back to your point, Mark,
coming from existing scenarios

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:45.720
or new scenarios--
a lot of times

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:47.900
they'll come with a
new initiative but then

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:50.990
all of a sudden, it'll blossom
into multiple initiatives

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:52.032
happening at once.

00:19:52.032 --> 00:19:53.990
MARK MANDEL: So I'm glad
you brought up on-prem

00:19:53.990 --> 00:19:56.780
because I wanted to ask you
about that data migration

00:19:56.780 --> 00:19:57.440
story.

00:19:57.440 --> 00:20:00.170
How are you seeing
customers taking their data

00:20:00.170 --> 00:20:02.178
and bringing it from, say
an on-prem environment

00:20:02.178 --> 00:20:03.470
and bringing it into the cloud?

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:05.540
I mean, that seems like a
big job in and of itself.

00:20:05.540 --> 00:20:06.950
BILL CREEKBAUM: It is a
big job in and of itself.

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:09.020
And there are
technical nuances that

00:20:09.020 --> 00:20:10.350
need to be considered, right?

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:11.960
The one that I run
into most often

00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:14.470
are differences in
data types, right?

00:20:14.470 --> 00:20:16.340
But what I see a
lot of customers

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:18.500
start with is just
like-- again, they

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:21.710
see dollar signs and the
opportunity for immediate cost

00:20:21.710 --> 00:20:22.210
savings.

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:23.760
And that's absolutely correct.

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:26.950
I mean, there can be a
tremendous and fast ROY

00:20:26.950 --> 00:20:30.080
by moving from a legacy
warehouse into BigQuery.

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:31.940
And what that means
is that, customers

00:20:31.940 --> 00:20:34.550
are oftentimes starting
with like a lift and shift.

00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:37.370
I just want take what
there, move it into BigQuery

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:38.710
and be done.

00:20:38.710 --> 00:20:42.010
And it's true, a
customer can realize

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:43.640
some quick savings that way.

00:20:43.640 --> 00:20:45.452
But so much is lost.

00:20:45.452 --> 00:20:47.410
And if you're going to
go through this effort--

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:49.120
and let's be honest,
this is an effort.

00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:52.420
No matter how much awesome
tooling both of our companies

00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:54.610
bring to the table,
it's still an effort.

00:20:54.610 --> 00:20:57.400
Customers that don't
take this inflection

00:20:57.400 --> 00:20:59.560
point to think
about the future, I

00:20:59.560 --> 00:21:01.620
think are missing a great
deal of opportunity.

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:04.840
I think the obvious example
is classic warehouse models

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:08.320
are heavily normalized,
which made a lot of sense

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:12.580
for the classic warehouse
based on old technologies,

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:15.620
old storage, old
compute and whatnot.

00:21:15.620 --> 00:21:17.560
But we all know that
when you move to BigQuery

00:21:17.560 --> 00:21:18.860
it's like, no, no.

00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:21.490
Well, seriously, to
normalize is to really take

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:23.390
advantage of the power.

00:21:23.390 --> 00:21:27.475
But it's such a complete
shift for DBAs and people

00:21:27.475 --> 00:21:31.250
that have been doing
this for 25 to 30 years.

00:21:31.250 --> 00:21:33.280
And for those that
just simply do

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:35.420
a lift and shift for that
immediate cost savings,

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:37.635
I think they're really
missing a great opportunity.

00:21:37.635 --> 00:21:39.760
And so a lot of times we'll
start off with the lift

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:42.650
and shift to get
immediate success.

00:21:42.650 --> 00:21:46.250
But then it immediately
moves into an evolution

00:21:46.250 --> 00:21:48.070
once you're in BigQuery
to start taking

00:21:48.070 --> 00:21:50.600
advantage of the data
structures and technologies that

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:51.100
are there.

00:21:51.100 --> 00:21:54.490
So it's not an either/or, but
it's a closely synchronous

00:21:54.490 --> 00:21:55.800
and, if you will.

00:21:55.800 --> 00:21:58.840
It goes lift and shift
immediately into evolution.

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:01.270
And that's where I think we
see a lot of benefits gained.

00:22:01.270 --> 00:22:02.895
GABI FERRARA: I
remember when I started

00:22:02.895 --> 00:22:07.180
working with a bit of big
data, I'm not that versed.

00:22:07.180 --> 00:22:13.270
Let's say that the fact of
being able to use BigQuery, just

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:16.260
put my data there and
it figure out indexes,

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:18.190
it figure out how many
servers it's going

00:22:18.190 --> 00:22:20.380
to take to spin off a query--

00:22:20.380 --> 00:22:24.790
that probably makes your
product more versatile

00:22:24.790 --> 00:22:27.880
taking advantage of
this kind of technology.

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:32.710
And you said this mind
shift for old school DBAs

00:22:32.710 --> 00:22:34.390
still has growing pains.

00:22:34.390 --> 00:22:37.593
Does that happen to you too?

00:22:37.593 --> 00:22:39.010
BILL CREEKBAUM:
It certainly does.

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:41.140
And I think,
especially customers

00:22:41.140 --> 00:22:44.210
that have been working with
Informatica for 20, 25 years,

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:49.030
there's a familiarity
with the common patterns.

00:22:49.030 --> 00:22:50.980
But sometimes those
common patterns

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:52.630
don't translate
well into the cloud

00:22:52.630 --> 00:22:54.410
and so there's a learning curve.

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:56.410
And I think one of the
things that Informatica

00:22:56.410 --> 00:22:58.480
is able to bring to the
table is that we help

00:22:58.480 --> 00:22:59.710
flatten that learning curve.

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:03.130
We can help take advantage
of the unique capabilities

00:23:03.130 --> 00:23:07.760
of the cloud faster because
from our customer's perspective,

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:10.090
they're still working with
this familiar toolset.

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:13.330
And yes, the technology behind
that we're connecting to

00:23:13.330 --> 00:23:13.900
differs.

00:23:13.900 --> 00:23:15.550
But we're able to
abstract it, so we're

00:23:15.550 --> 00:23:18.160
able to get some
really fast adoption.

00:23:18.160 --> 00:23:20.320
But once you do that
fast adoption-- remember,

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:22.663
a few moments go I was
talking about we also

00:23:22.663 --> 00:23:24.580
want to make sure that
the customers can truly

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:27.850
take advantage of the
characteristics of Google Cloud

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:29.150
Platform in BigQuery.

00:23:29.150 --> 00:23:31.200
So for example, we
talked about ETL earlier

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:34.390
but let's talk about ELT,
extract load transform.

00:23:34.390 --> 00:23:36.160
We want to seriously
take advantage

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:38.650
of that kind of
capability in BigQuery.

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:41.350
So using our intelligent
cloud services

00:23:41.350 --> 00:23:44.560
to actually load
data into BigQuery

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:46.660
and then process
the transformation

00:23:46.660 --> 00:23:48.850
inside BigQuery-- we're
able to actually push down

00:23:48.850 --> 00:23:51.610
the entire job into
BigQuery so that we're

00:23:51.610 --> 00:23:54.710
using the BigQuery engine to
do all the transformations.

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:56.210
And that's got a
couple of benefits.

00:23:56.210 --> 00:23:57.840
Number one, you
just talked about.

00:23:57.840 --> 00:24:00.190
It's a serverless
scale out environment

00:24:00.190 --> 00:24:02.980
with tremendous compute
power that you're

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:05.110
able to spin up on demand.

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:08.810
That means Informatica is able
to take advantage of that.

00:24:08.810 --> 00:24:12.820
And so we're able to deliver
a crazy amount of performance

00:24:12.820 --> 00:24:13.870
with ease.

00:24:13.870 --> 00:24:17.622
And our common customers are
benefiting greatly from that.

00:24:17.622 --> 00:24:18.580
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:24:18.580 --> 00:24:20.050
I'm kind of curious
as well too, obviously

00:24:20.050 --> 00:24:22.750
you've worked with a variety
of people who do these big data

00:24:22.750 --> 00:24:24.310
and analytics platforms.

00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:27.605
If you could tell anyone who
works in big data and analytics

00:24:27.605 --> 00:24:29.230
one thing that they
should all be doing

00:24:29.230 --> 00:24:31.870
or one mistake they should
potentially not make?

00:24:31.870 --> 00:24:33.492
That's broadly generalized.

00:24:33.492 --> 00:24:35.950
What would you suggest as maybe
the best practice you would

00:24:35.950 --> 00:24:37.460
love to get out into the world?

00:24:37.460 --> 00:24:40.530
BILL CREEKBAUM: Oh, Mark,
that's such a loaded question.

00:24:40.530 --> 00:24:42.020
MARK MANDEL: That's
why I asked it.

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:44.388
BILL CREEKBAUM: Can't
I have like 10 not one?

00:24:44.388 --> 00:24:46.180
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, we
can give it a couple.

00:24:46.180 --> 00:24:47.598
We've got some time.

00:24:47.598 --> 00:24:49.640
BILL CREEKBAUM: I think
I'd probably boil it down

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:51.882
into three very distinct
recommendations.

00:24:51.882 --> 00:24:54.340
Number one is, you've got to
take stock of what you've got.

00:24:54.340 --> 00:24:56.650
I think companies are
completely surprised

00:24:56.650 --> 00:24:59.230
by the amount of data assets
that they have in every nook

00:24:59.230 --> 00:25:00.700
and cranny of their enterprise.

00:25:00.700 --> 00:25:03.730
And you really want to take
a good inventory of what

00:25:03.730 --> 00:25:06.565
you have, determine what's
important, prioritizing,

00:25:06.565 --> 00:25:07.560
and categories.

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:08.920
That's kind of step one.

00:25:08.920 --> 00:25:11.630
But maybe most importantly,
you need to set goals.

00:25:11.630 --> 00:25:14.240
And I know that sounds
basic and it sounds obvious,

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:17.440
but more often than not, I'm
not seeing clear goals set.

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:19.510
So A, we have no
idea when we ever

00:25:19.510 --> 00:25:21.610
finish because we don't
know where we're headed.

00:25:21.610 --> 00:25:23.950
And when we do get
there, was it successful?

00:25:23.950 --> 00:25:25.240
Was it not?

00:25:25.240 --> 00:25:26.160
We don't know.

00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:28.420
And by setting
goals, you set focus.

00:25:28.420 --> 00:25:31.900
You set your guiding star that
everyone aligns and marches

00:25:31.900 --> 00:25:32.720
towards.

00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:34.720
Which then leads into the
third one and that is,

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:35.950
you've got to develop a team.

00:25:35.950 --> 00:25:39.440
And again, it seems obvious
but maybe not in the way

00:25:39.440 --> 00:25:41.190
that you're thinking about it.

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:45.400
I would recommend companies
to form a migration SWAT team.

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:48.680
And this is a team that is well
versed both in the technology

00:25:48.680 --> 00:25:50.810
and the business goals.

00:25:50.810 --> 00:25:52.730
Because they need to
be the leaders, right?

00:25:52.730 --> 00:25:55.790
Because they're going to need
to get people to follow them

00:25:55.790 --> 00:25:58.118
on this journey into the cloud.

00:25:58.118 --> 00:25:59.660
And for many people,
especially those

00:25:59.660 --> 00:26:01.910
that are comfortable with
the familiarity of doing

00:26:01.910 --> 00:26:03.780
it the old way, they're
going to ask why.

00:26:03.780 --> 00:26:06.890
And you want this team to
be able to not only execute,

00:26:06.890 --> 00:26:09.630
but to also inspire the
confidence in moving

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:10.940
to the cloud.

00:26:10.940 --> 00:26:13.755
And so when you combine
that with clear goals

00:26:13.755 --> 00:26:15.380
and a really good
understanding of what

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:18.740
you have in place today, I just
think it sets an enterprise up

00:26:18.740 --> 00:26:20.030
for success.

00:26:20.030 --> 00:26:22.430
GABI FERRARA: Talking
about innovations

00:26:22.430 --> 00:26:27.160
and easy to use technologies
is good, but also brings up,

00:26:27.160 --> 00:26:29.841
with great powers, greater
responsibilities, right?

00:26:29.841 --> 00:26:32.930
[LAUGH TRACK]

00:26:32.930 --> 00:26:37.520
So how does GDPR and
those kind of regulations

00:26:37.520 --> 00:26:40.850
ensure they're working
with Informatica?

00:26:40.850 --> 00:26:44.450
Because one thing
is to be GDPR ready,

00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:46.525
and then thing to
be GDPR compliant.

00:26:46.525 --> 00:26:47.650
BILL CREEKBAUM: Absolutely.

00:26:47.650 --> 00:26:48.608
GABI FERRARA: You know?

00:26:48.608 --> 00:26:50.100
So how do you deal with that?

00:26:50.100 --> 00:26:51.850
BILL CREEKBAUM:
Well, again, I don't

00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:53.308
want to sound like
a broken record,

00:26:53.308 --> 00:26:56.830
but because we deliver a
platform that integrates

00:26:56.830 --> 00:27:00.680
integration, governance,
security, metadata management,

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:03.200
discovery as part of
the whole platform,

00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:05.210
it's a little bit
easier for us to deliver

00:27:05.210 --> 00:27:09.990
on the realization of being
GDPR compliant, not just ready.

00:27:09.990 --> 00:27:13.290
For example, the
right to be forgotten.

00:27:13.290 --> 00:27:14.900
It's a difficult problem, right?

00:27:14.900 --> 00:27:16.880
I mean, if you think
like a global enterprise

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:19.850
with many subsidiaries,
you probably

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:22.850
have PIIs sitting all
over the globe managed

00:27:22.850 --> 00:27:24.980
by many different
business units.

00:27:24.980 --> 00:27:27.980
But the right to be forgotten
doesn't care about such silos.

00:27:27.980 --> 00:27:30.320
It's like, if I want to
be forgotten, that's it.

00:27:30.320 --> 00:27:32.180
You need it forget me.

00:27:32.180 --> 00:27:35.210
And so we have a product that's
called Secure Source that

00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:37.790
allows a compliance
officer to log in

00:27:37.790 --> 00:27:40.760
and actually says, show
me on all the locations

00:27:40.760 --> 00:27:43.730
within my enterprise
where PII exists.

00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:45.530
And then from there,
again, it's like, OK.

00:27:45.530 --> 00:27:47.120
I have a road map
of where I need

00:27:47.120 --> 00:27:50.630
to go so that when somebody
chooses to be forgotten,

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:52.640
I'm not guessing or hoping.

00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:54.765
I'm able to know.

00:27:54.765 --> 00:27:56.390
And it's definitely
one of the benefits

00:27:56.390 --> 00:28:00.590
of having that unified
platform that's

00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:02.350
integrated as a
complete solution

00:28:02.350 --> 00:28:03.350
because it enables that.

00:28:03.350 --> 00:28:05.900
And things like GDPR and CCPA--

00:28:05.900 --> 00:28:08.540
I mean, they codify much of
what we've talked about, right?

00:28:08.540 --> 00:28:11.500
We've talked about
security and governance

00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:14.270
since we've began our
conversation a short while ago.

00:28:14.270 --> 00:28:17.368
And this kind of codifies
the requirements.

00:28:17.368 --> 00:28:19.160
But I think in some
sense, every enterprise

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:21.010
worries about this
regardless of regulation.

00:28:21.010 --> 00:28:23.510
MARK MANDEL: Speaking of things
like personally identifiable

00:28:23.510 --> 00:28:27.158
information and other potential
high-risk information storage,

00:28:27.158 --> 00:28:29.450
do you have best practices
or things that people should

00:28:29.450 --> 00:28:31.460
be aware of when looking
at that kind of data that

00:28:31.460 --> 00:28:32.390
could potentially be high-risk?

00:28:32.390 --> 00:28:33.515
BILL CREEKBAUM: Absolutely.

00:28:33.515 --> 00:28:36.950
I mean, we started off talking
about modernizing your data

00:28:36.950 --> 00:28:38.190
infrastructure to BigQuery.

00:28:38.190 --> 00:28:41.750
So let's look at it from the
construct of that journey,

00:28:41.750 --> 00:28:42.680
right?

00:28:42.680 --> 00:28:46.640
And we talked about setting
goals and building a team

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:48.050
to be successful.

00:28:48.050 --> 00:28:50.930
Well, part of being
successful also quick wins.

00:28:50.930 --> 00:28:55.030
You want to build momentum
based off of success.

00:28:55.030 --> 00:28:58.910
So one of things that I've
seen successful companies do

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:02.150
is when they start their
migrations into the cloud,

00:29:02.150 --> 00:29:05.720
let's not start off with
the tables full of PII

00:29:05.720 --> 00:29:07.323
because that
radically complicates

00:29:07.323 --> 00:29:09.990
the scenario because now you got
to get your IT people involved,

00:29:09.990 --> 00:29:11.573
you get your security
people involved,

00:29:11.573 --> 00:29:13.500
you get the governance
people involved.

00:29:13.500 --> 00:29:16.650
So sometimes it's best to know
where your PII data is not,

00:29:16.650 --> 00:29:17.150
right?

00:29:17.150 --> 00:29:19.700
And so, by using
your data catalog,

00:29:19.700 --> 00:29:22.280
by using Informatica's
data catalog,

00:29:22.280 --> 00:29:24.120
you can see and not guess.

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:26.600
It's like, OK, this
table's got a bunch of PII.

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:27.680
It's a lot of value here.

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:29.000
But you know what,
let's do that maybe

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:30.240
in phase two or phase three.

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:32.365
Let's come and take a look
these other data objects

00:29:32.365 --> 00:29:35.420
that has a lot of
business value below PII.

00:29:35.420 --> 00:29:39.140
And that's going to allow me
to migrate to BigQuery rapidly,

00:29:39.140 --> 00:29:43.280
get success, build a new use
case, and now I can repeat.

00:29:43.280 --> 00:29:45.920
And build on that success
and gain that momentum.

00:29:45.920 --> 00:29:47.960
Probably almost like
the opposite answer

00:29:47.960 --> 00:29:50.002
what you expected expecting
but sometimes knowing

00:29:50.002 --> 00:29:52.365
where your PII is not
is very beneficial.

00:29:52.365 --> 00:29:53.240
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:29:53.240 --> 00:29:54.320
All right, cool.

00:29:54.320 --> 00:29:56.450
So we're coming up on
time here, unfortunately.

00:29:56.450 --> 00:29:58.250
But before we go,
Bill, is there anything

00:29:58.250 --> 00:30:00.260
that we've missed or you
want to mention or make sure

00:30:00.260 --> 00:30:02.050
that you get out there
so people can hear it?

00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:04.125
BILL CREEKBAUM: You know, Mark,
so number one, thanks to you

00:30:04.125 --> 00:30:04.625
and Gabi.

00:30:04.625 --> 00:30:06.830
I've had a great time
chatting with you both.

00:30:06.830 --> 00:30:09.270
Yeah, I would say that to our
customers listening today,

00:30:09.270 --> 00:30:11.270
check out the partnership
between our companies.

00:30:11.270 --> 00:30:12.090
It's fantastic.

00:30:12.090 --> 00:30:15.500
I truly enjoy working
with the team at Google

00:30:15.500 --> 00:30:18.050
and together we're doing
some great solutions

00:30:18.050 --> 00:30:19.040
for our customers.

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:21.120
And it's fun to be in
market with you guys.

00:30:21.120 --> 00:30:21.662
So thank you.

00:30:21.662 --> 00:30:22.537
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:30:22.537 --> 00:30:24.350
Well, thanks for being
a wonderful partner.

00:30:24.350 --> 00:30:27.830
Well, Bill, thank you so
much for joining us today

00:30:27.830 --> 00:30:28.455
on the podcast.

00:30:28.455 --> 00:30:30.080
BILL CREEKBAUM: My
pleasure, thank you.

00:30:30.080 --> 00:30:32.060
MARK MANDEL: Huge thanks
to Bill for joining

