00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:05.710
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So when
you create an App Engine app,

00:31:05.710 --> 00:31:07.222
you get a domain
that comes with it.

00:31:07.222 --> 00:31:07.930
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:31:07.930 --> 00:31:09.972
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Usually
a pretty generic domain

00:31:09.972 --> 00:31:12.010
based on the project
name and then App Engine.

00:31:12.010 --> 00:31:14.475
But of course, you can use
your own custom domains.

00:31:14.475 --> 00:31:15.850
And I think a lot
of people, they

00:31:15.850 --> 00:31:18.160
want to do that because
custom domains are nice.

00:31:18.160 --> 00:31:20.220
But when you're using a
custom domain, sometimes

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:21.970
a lot of times you
need to use subdomains.

00:31:21.970 --> 00:31:25.090
How the heck do
you use subdomains

00:31:25.090 --> 00:31:27.040
with a custom domain
in App Engine?

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:27.790
MARK MANDEL: Whoo.

00:31:27.790 --> 00:31:28.910
That's a good question.

00:31:28.910 --> 00:31:31.780
So there is actually a
lot of really cool ways

00:31:31.780 --> 00:31:34.060
you can configure
subdomains with App

00:31:34.060 --> 00:31:36.640
Engine in both static
and dynamic ways,

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:37.970
which is super nice.

00:31:37.970 --> 00:31:41.470
So for a static point of view,
you can set up a dispatch.yaml.

00:31:41.470 --> 00:31:44.710
It's a configuration file
wherein you can configure,

00:31:44.710 --> 00:31:46.173
actually, a variety of stuff.

00:31:46.173 --> 00:31:47.590
Not only just
subdomains, but also

00:31:47.590 --> 00:31:50.380
pods and some other things
to particular services.

00:31:50.380 --> 00:31:53.712
So services are individual
deployments inside App Engine.

00:31:53.712 --> 00:31:55.420
Like if you had
different APIs endpoints,

00:31:55.420 --> 00:31:56.560
you might want to do
different services.

00:31:56.560 --> 00:31:59.218
Then you can have versions
within those services as well.

00:31:59.218 --> 00:32:01.510
So you can set that up within
your dispatch.yaml to say

00:32:01.510 --> 00:32:05.350
like, oh, I want
[? dog.food.bar ?] I

00:32:05.350 --> 00:32:07.810
want that dot com to go
to my default service.

00:32:07.810 --> 00:32:12.273
But [? cat.food.bar ?] I
want to go to my cat service.

00:32:12.273 --> 00:32:13.690
And that's pretty
straightforward.

00:32:13.690 --> 00:32:16.743
It's just a yaml file and
it is pretty easy to setup.

00:32:16.743 --> 00:32:18.910
That being said, if you
want to get dynamic with it,

00:32:18.910 --> 00:32:20.080
which some people do.

00:32:20.080 --> 00:32:23.057
We use it for some things
and it might be a bit easier.

00:32:23.057 --> 00:32:24.890
You can set that up
with Wild Cards as well.

00:32:24.890 --> 00:32:26.470
So you can do that
to the lower level

00:32:26.470 --> 00:32:27.595
down in your dispatch.yaml.

00:32:27.595 --> 00:32:29.137
But if you just want
to do it simply,

00:32:29.137 --> 00:32:30.640
you don't even need
a dispatch.yaml.

00:32:30.640 --> 00:32:32.560
You can set up
your custom domain

00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:34.270
with, basically, a Wild Card.

00:32:34.270 --> 00:32:36.140
So you might be like
[? star.food.com ?]

00:32:36.140 --> 00:32:38.640
and you can set that up in your
custom domain configuration.

00:32:38.640 --> 00:32:40.750
And then potentially also
have a Wild Card for your SSL

00:32:40.750 --> 00:32:42.670
if you have an SSL, which
you probably should.

00:32:42.670 --> 00:32:45.130
And what's nice there
is what will happen

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:49.240
there is if a person goes to
a subdomain, writes a Wild

00:32:49.240 --> 00:32:51.400
Card-- could be any
subdomain, App Engine

00:32:51.400 --> 00:32:54.910
will actually look for a
version or a service that

00:32:54.910 --> 00:32:57.250
matches the same name
as that subdomain.

00:32:57.250 --> 00:32:59.560
If you go to a
[? dog.food.com, ?] it will go

00:32:59.560 --> 00:33:03.590
look for a service named "dog"
and it will then serve that.

00:33:03.590 --> 00:33:06.317
So that be a really handy
way if you have subdomains

00:33:06.317 --> 00:33:07.900
that come up on a
regular basis, maybe

00:33:07.900 --> 00:33:09.610
you have snapshots
of documentation

00:33:09.610 --> 00:33:11.027
that you want
[INAUDIBLE] version,

00:33:11.027 --> 00:33:13.480
something like that, that
come up every regular period.

00:33:13.480 --> 00:33:14.980
You can set up something
dynamic like that

00:33:14.980 --> 00:33:17.355
so that all you have to do is
create a new either version

00:33:17.355 --> 00:33:20.050
or service that matches
the subdomain that you have

00:33:20.050 --> 00:33:21.230
and then that'll get served.

00:33:21.230 --> 00:33:22.650
And if App Engine can't
find that subdomain

00:33:22.650 --> 00:33:24.760
it will just send it back to
default, which is fine too.

00:33:24.760 --> 00:33:27.177
So you can do some really nice
dynamic stuff that way too.

00:33:27.177 --> 00:33:28.430
So you have lots of options.

00:33:28.430 --> 00:33:29.830
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So if
you have the Wild Card,

00:33:29.830 --> 00:33:31.630
you obviously get all
the subdomains you want.

00:33:31.630 --> 00:33:33.547
And then from there, you
can either statically

00:33:33.547 --> 00:33:35.740
configure them, saying
which ones you want.

00:33:35.740 --> 00:33:38.230
Or you can do it
dynamically and then pull up

00:33:38.230 --> 00:33:40.480
different versions, which I
think you made a great use

00:33:40.480 --> 00:33:42.395
case for when you said,
hey, you might have

00:33:42.395 --> 00:33:44.020
different versions
that are in the past

00:33:44.020 --> 00:33:45.750
and you want to still
have references to those.

00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:46.150
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:33:46.150 --> 00:33:48.010
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So you
kind of have two great ways

00:33:48.010 --> 00:33:50.530
to continue hosting content
all from a single App Engine

00:33:50.530 --> 00:33:51.220
project.

00:33:51.220 --> 00:33:52.750
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, and it's
kind of like that whole thing

00:33:52.750 --> 00:33:54.415
where it's, convention
for configuration--

00:33:54.415 --> 00:33:55.870
you know one way,
with the Wild Cards.

00:33:55.870 --> 00:33:57.370
Or you can have
static configuration

00:33:57.370 --> 00:33:58.540
using your dispatch.yaml.

